      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the science.\n\nWhen the Maillard reaction reaches its optimal temperature range (around 140°C to 180°C), the amino acids and reducing sugars begin to break down and recombine in a complex process. At this point, the molecules start to form new bonds, leading to the formation of new flavor compounds, browning, and aromas.\n\nOne of the key events in the Maillard reaction is the formation of melanoidins, which are large, complex molecules that are responsible for the characteristic brown color and flavor of cooked foods. This occurs when amino acids and reducing sugars react with each other to form a series of intermediate compounds, which eventually lead to the formation of melanoidins.\n\nThink of it like a game of molecular dominoes, where each domino (molecule) falls into place, triggering the next reaction, and so on. The resulting melanoidins are highly reactive and can react with other molecules to form new flavor compounds, contributing to the development of the characteristic flavors and aromas of cooked foods.\n\nIt's a bit like a cleverly designed trap set by a goblin, where each molecule is carefully positioned to lead to the next",
